  "lineage": "Traditional Arab coffeehouse storytelling of the Levant, Egypt and the wider Arab world, in which the hakawati ('teller') serialises long popular epics — the Sirat Bani Hilal, the exploits of Antar ibn Shaddad, Baybars, and tales from the Thousand and One Nights — across nightly installments, often reading from a chapbook while embellishing in performance. Seated on a raised chair with a cane or sword as prop, the teller times cliffhangers to the audience and the coffee trade; the art was near-extinct by the late twentieth century and is associated with figures such as Damascus's Abu Shadi (Rashid al-Hallak). Delivery is expressive spoken narration marked by direct address, formulaic openings and pious interjection.",
